window.READING_TESTS={1:{"id": 1, "module": "reading", "vol": 9, "sections": [{"number": 1, "html": "<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 1–6</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Choose <b>TRUE</b> if the statement agrees with the information given in the text, choose <b>FALSE</b> if the statement contradicts the information, or choose <b>NOT GIVEN</b> if there is no information on this.</p>\n<div data-render-choice=\"1\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"2\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"3\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"4\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"5\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"6\"></div>\n<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 7–13</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Complete the notes below.</p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Write <b>ONE WORD ONLY</b> from the passage for each answer.</p>\n<p class=\"\"><b>Baobabs under threat</b></p>\n<p class=\"\">Reasons why Madagascar&#x27;s baobabs are declining in number:</p>\n<p class=\"\">Morondava area</p>\n<p>trees may be falling down because of  <span class=\"qnum\">7</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"7\" aria-label=\"Câu 7\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\">  from nearby fields</p>\n<p>lone trees are at risk from  <span class=\"qnum\">8</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"8\" aria-label=\"Câu 8\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"></p>\n<p class=\"\">Forest areas</p>\n<p>land clearance: mainly by burning carried out to create space for <span class=\"qnum\">9</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"9\" aria-label=\"Câu 9\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> activity</p>\n<p>practice of  <span class=\"qnum\">10</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"10\" aria-label=\"Câu 10\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\">  makes seed germination difficult</p>\n<p>disappearance of certain  <span class=\"qnum\">11</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"11\" aria-label=\"Câu 11\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> which dispersed baobab seeds</p>\n<p class=\"\">Jim Bond’s efforts to record information about the baobab:</p>\n<p>making <span class=\"qnum\">12</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"12\" aria-label=\"Câu 12\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> of areas of relatively undamaged baobab forest</p>\n<p>producing a  <span class=\"qnum\">13</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"13\" aria-label=\"Câu 13\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> showing importance of baobab in local culture.</p>"}, {"number": 2, "html": "<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 14–20</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Reading Passage 2 has seven paragraphs.</p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below.</p>\n<h3>List of Headings</h3><div class=\"bank heading-bank\"><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"i\"><b>i</b> Training coin makers</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"ii\"><b>ii</b> A form of regional promotion</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"iii\"><b>iii</b> More gold than silver in Greece</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"iv\"><b>iv</b> Calculating what a coin was worth</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"v\"><b>v</b> A simpler, more efficient way of trading</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"vi\"><b>vi</b> Putting a face to famous historical names</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"vii\"><b>vii</b> An uncertain future</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"viii\"><b>viii</b> Careless workmanship caused breakages</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"ix\"><b>ix</b> Unchanging coins signalled trustworthiness</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"x\"><b>x</b> Reading the writing on coins</button><button class=\"token\" draggable=\"true\" data-token=\"xi\"><b>xi</b> Significant records of past societies</button></div>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"14\"><span class=\"qnum\">14</span>Paragraph 1 <select data-q=\"14\" aria-label=\"Câu 14\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"15\"><span class=\"qnum\">15</span>Paragraph 2 <select data-q=\"15\" aria-label=\"Câu 15\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"16\"><span class=\"qnum\">16</span>Paragraph 3 <select data-q=\"16\" aria-label=\"Câu 16\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"17\"><span class=\"qnum\">17</span>Paragraph 4 <select data-q=\"17\" aria-label=\"Câu 17\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"18\"><span class=\"qnum\">18</span>Paragraph 5 <select data-q=\"18\" aria-label=\"Câu 18\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"19\"><span class=\"qnum\">19</span>Paragraph 6 <select data-q=\"19\" aria-label=\"Câu 19\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"20\"><span class=\"qnum\">20</span>Paragraph 7 <select data-q=\"20\" aria-label=\"Câu 20\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"i\">i</option><option value=\"ii\">ii</option><option value=\"iii\">iii</option><option value=\"iv\">iv</option><option value=\"v\">v</option><option value=\"vi\">vi</option><option value=\"vii\">vii</option><option value=\"viii\">viii</option><option value=\"ix\">ix</option><option value=\"x\">x</option><option value=\"xi\">xi</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 21–22</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Choose TWO correct answers.</p>\n<div data-render-pair=\"21\"></div>\n<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 23–26</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Complete the summary below.</p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Write <b>ONE WORD ONLY </b>from the passage for each answer.</p>\n<p class=\"\"><b>Greek traders and coins</b></p>\n<p>The ancient Greek <span class=\"qnum\">23</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"23\" aria-label=\"Câu 23\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> was made up of many city-states which were spread over a large area. Many Greeks were seafarers who travelled the seas trading. Before coins, they had to do this through <span class=\"qnum\">24</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"24\" aria-label=\"Câu 24\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\">. This meant that, whenever they set out, they had to carry a lot of goods, which could get damaged on the voyages. The Greeks liked coins; they were made of metal, and the weather and sea had little effect on them. The Greeks had a plentiful supply of <span class=\"qnum\">25</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"25\" aria-label=\"Câu 25\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> which they mined themselves, but were able to avoid the problem of <span class=\"qnum\">26</span><input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"26\" aria-label=\"Câu 26\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> which the Spanish encountered much later, after they allowed too much gold from the New World to enter their economy.</p>"}, {"number": 3, "html": "<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 27–33</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Reading Passage 3 has nine paragraphs, <b>A-I.</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\"><i>Which paragraph contains the following information?</i></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Choose the correct letter, <b>A-I</b>, in boxes <b>27-33</b>. </p>\n<p class=\"instruction\"><i><b>NB</b></i><i>  You may use any letter more than once.</i></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"27\"><span class=\"qnum\">27</span>details of an experiment in taste comparison <select data-q=\"27\" aria-label=\"Câu 27\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"28\"><span class=\"qnum\">28</span>examples of medications that could be improved using compounds that remove bitterness <select data-q=\"28\" aria-label=\"Câu 28\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"29\"><span class=\"qnum\">29</span>a biological reason why some people don&#x27;t like bitter-tasting foods <select data-q=\"29\" aria-label=\"Câu 29\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"30\"><span class=\"qnum\">30</span>an explanation of how the taste process works <select data-q=\"30\" aria-label=\"Câu 30\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"31\"><span class=\"qnum\">31</span>why bitterness is more interesting commercially than other tastes <select data-q=\"31\" aria-label=\"Câu 31\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"32\"><span class=\"qnum\">32</span>an example of how compounds that remove bitterness could indirectly benefit health <select data-q=\"32\" aria-label=\"Câu 32\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"matching-row\" data-drop=\"33\"><span class=\"qnum\">33</span>a reason why people have different taste preferences from animals <select data-q=\"33\" aria-label=\"Câu 33\"><option value=\"\">Chọn</option><option value=\"A\">A</option><option value=\"B\">B</option><option value=\"C\">C</option><option value=\"D\">D</option><option value=\"E\">E</option><option value=\"F\">F</option><option value=\"G\">G</option><option value=\"H\">H</option><option value=\"I\">I</option></select></p>\n<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 34–38</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\"><i>Complete the sentences below.</i></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\">Write <b>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS </b>from the passage for each answer. </p>\n<p><span class=\"qnum\">34</span>The grapefruit used to make drinks is chosen because it contains a smaller amount of a substance called  <input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"34\" aria-label=\"Câu 34\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"></p>\n<p><span class=\"qnum\">35</span>Animals associate a bitter taste with  <input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"35\" aria-label=\"Câu 35\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\">  plants.</p>\n<p><span class=\"qnum\">36</span>Our varying degree of sensitivity to bitter tastes is thought to be <input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"36\" aria-label=\"Câu 36\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> </p>\n<p><span class=\"qnum\">37</span>People who are extremely aware of bitter tastes are called  <input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"37\" aria-label=\"Câu 37\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"></p>\n<p><span class=\"qnum\">38</span>Receptors inside the  <input class=\"gap\" data-q=\"38\" aria-label=\"Câu 38\" autocomplete=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\"> on the tongue detect the taste of food and drink.</p>\n<p class=\"subheading\"><b>Questions 39–40</b></p>\n<p class=\"instruction\"><i>Choose the correct answer.</i></p>\n<div data-render-choice=\"39\"></div>\n<div data-render-choice=\"40\"></div>"}], "passages": [{"number": 1, "title": "The Baobabs of Madagascar", "subtitle": "<i>The ancient baobabs of Madagascar are some of the largest and most distinctive trees in the world, but they face an uncertain future</i>"}, {"number": 2, "title": "Coins – the first form of money", "subtitle": ""}, {"number": 3, "title": "Creating a Better Grapefruit", "subtitle": "<i>By blocking the right taste receptors, biotech researchers turn bitter into sweet</i>"}], "singles": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "All of the baobab species found on Madagascar are unique to the island.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Adanson and Grandidier were the first foreign botanists to visit Madagascar.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "The baobab can be found in most areas of Madagascar.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "The landscape around the Avenue des Baobabs is the result of agricultural development.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "The baobab provides useful products for the local people.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "There is general agreement about the reasons for baobabs falling down.", "options": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"], "values": ["TRUE", "FALSE", "NOT GIVEN"]}, {"n": 39, "prompt": "The G protein identified by Robert Margolskee", "options": ["is responsible for transmitting bitter tastes.", "causes dangerous reactions in the cells of the tongue.", "has a similar function to sight and smell receptors.", "can be found only in sweetness and umami."]}, {"n": 40, "prompt": "AMP is an organic compound which is", "options": ["found only in breast milk.", "bitter-tasting when added to food", "able to reduce some bitter tastes", "found in bitter taste receptors"]}], "pairs": [{"n": 21, "prompt": "Which TWO of these comments about ancient coins are made by the writer of the text?", "options": ["There is no record of the names of the coin makers.", "Coins were not popular as payment for traded objects.", "Coins with the same design often looked quite different.", "Greek coins were more beautiful in design than Roman coins.", "At times, too many coins were produced in Greece."]}], "answers": {"1": ["FALSE"], "2": ["NOT GIVEN"], "3": ["FALSE"], "4": ["TRUE"], "5": ["TRUE"], "6": ["FALSE"], "7": ["WATER"], "8": ["CYCLONES"], "9": ["FARMING"], "10": ["LOGGING"], "11": ["ANIMALS"], "12": ["MAPS"], "13": ["BOOK"], "14": ["vii"], "15": ["xi"], "16": ["v"], "17": ["iv"], "18": ["ii"], "19": ["ix"], "20": ["vi"], "21": ["A/C"], "22": ["C/A"], "23": ["civilisation", "civilization"], "24": ["barter"], "25": ["silver"], "26": ["inflation"], "27": ["B"], "28": ["H"], "29": ["E"], "30": ["F"], "31": ["C"], "32": ["H"], "33": ["D"], "34": ["naringin"], "35": ["poisonous"], "36": ["genetic"], "37": ["supertasters"], "38": ["taste buds"], "39": ["A"], "40": ["C"]}, "explanations": {"1": "<p><b>1. F</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>All of the baobab species found on Madagascar are unique to the island.</i></p><p>(Tất cả các loài cây bao báp được tìm thấy ở Madagascar đều là loài đặc hữu của hòn đảo này.)</p><p><u><i>Đoạn chứa đáp án</i></u>:</p><p>Of the eight known species, six are found only there, while a seventh has made its way across the Mozambique Channel from mainland Africa with a certain amount of human help.</p><p>Trong số tám loài đã biết, sáu loài chỉ được tìm thấy ở đó, trong khi loài thứ bảy đã vượt qua eo biển Mozambique từ lục địa châu Phi với một số trợ giúp của con người.</p><p>Giải thích: Có 8 loài, 6 loài đặc hữu, 1 loài từ châu Phi sang. Không phải tất cả đều đặc hữu.</p>", "2": "<p><b>2. NG</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Adanson and Grandidier were the first foreign botanists to visit Madagascar.</i></p><p>(Adanson và Grandidier là những nhà thực vật học nước ngoài đầu tiên đến Madagascar.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Known as renala – ‘mother of the forest’ - in the local Malagasy* language, these trees belong to the tallest baobab species in the world: Adansonia grandidieri, named after two great 19th-century French botanists, Michel Adanson and Alfred Grandidier.</p><p>Được gọi là renala – nghĩa là “mẹ của rừng” trong ngôn ngữ Malagasy địa phương – những cây này thuộc loài bao báp cao nhất thế giới: Adansonia grandidieri, được đặt tên theo hai nhà thực vật học người Pháp nổi tiếng thế kỷ 19 là Michel Adanson và Alfred Grandidier.</p><p>Giải thích: Đoạn chỉ nói họ là nhà thực vật học nổi tiếng, không nói họ là những người đầu tiên đến Madagascar.</p>", "3": "<p><b>3.F</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The baobab can be found in most areas of Madagascar.</i></p><p>(Cây bao báp có thể được tìm thấy ở hầu hết các khu vực của Madagascar.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Baobabs have religious and spiritual significance for many Malagasy tribes, although the majority of the island’s population will probably never see one – they only grow along the western margin of the island and not in the more populous central highlands</p><p>Cây bao báp có ý nghĩa tôn giáo và tâm linh đối với nhiều bộ tộc Malagasy, mặc dù phần lớn dân cư trên đảo có lẽ sẽ không bao giờ nhìn thấy chúng – bởi vì chúng chỉ mọc dọc theo bờ phía tây của hòn đảo và không xuất hiện ở vùng cao nguyên trung tâm đông dân hơn.</p><p>Giải thích: Chúng chỉ mọc ở vùng phía tây, không phải hầu hết các khu vực.</p>", "4": "<p><b>4.T</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The landscape around the Avenue des Baobabs is the result of agricultural development.</i></p><p>(Cảnh quan xung quanh Đại lộ Bao báp là kết quả của sự phát triển nông nghiệp.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>While the trees themselves are natural enough, the surrounding landscape was created by humans. Back in the early 20th century, much of the Morondava plain was cleared for rice and sugar-cane fields.</p><p>Mặc dù bản thân những cây này hoàn toàn là tự nhiên, nhưng cảnh quan xung quanh lại do con người tạo ra. Vào đầu thế kỷ 20, phần lớn đồng bằng Morondava đã bị phát quang để làm ruộng lúa và trồng mía.</p><p>Đáp án: TRUE</p>", "5": "<p><b>5.T</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The baobab provides useful products for the local people</i></p><p>(Cây bao báp cung cấp các sản phẩm hữu ích cho người dân địa phương.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>However, many baobabs were spared because they are valuable as a food source and renewable building material, or they simply survived the fires that were set to clear the dry deciduous forest around them.</p><p>Tuy nhiên, nhiều cây bao báp đã được giữ lại vì chúng có giá trị như một nguồn thực phẩm và là vật liệu xây dựng có thể tái tạo, hoặc đơn giản là chúng đã sống sót sau những đám cháy được đốt lên để dọn sạch khu rừng rụng lá khô xung quanh.</p><p>Giải thích: chúng có giá trị làm thực phẩm và vật liệu xây dựng.</p>", "6": "<p><b>6.  F</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>There is general agreement about the reasons for baobabs falling down.</i></p><p>(Có sự đồng thuận chung về lý do tại sao cây bao báp lại đổ.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The exact cause of the falling baobabs is uncertain, but some people think that the water used to irrigate the nearby fields is partly to blame.</p><p>Nguyên nhân chính xác khiến các cây bao báp bị đổ vẫn chưa rõ, nhưng một số người cho rằng lượng nước dùng để tưới các cánh đồng gần đó có thể là một phần nguyên nhân.</p><p>Giải thích: Nguyên nhân chưa chắc chắn, có nhiều ý kiến khác nhau. Không có sự đồng thuận.</p>", "7": "<p><b>7. water</b></p><p>Câu hỏi:  <i>trees may be falling down because of ______from nearby fields</i></p><p>(Cây có thể bị đổ do_______ từ các cánh đồng gần đó)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The exact cause of the falling baobabs is uncertain, but some people think that the water used to irrigate the nearby fields is partly to blame.</p><p>Nguyên nhân chính xác khiến các cây bao báp bị đổ vẫn chưa rõ, nhưng một số người cho rằng nước dùng để tưới các cánh đồng gần đó có thể là một phần nguyên nhân gây ra việc này.</p>", "8": "<p><b>8.  cyclones</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>lone trees are at risk from______</i></p><p>(Cây đơn lẻ có nguy cơ bị_____)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Baobabs are adapted to the annual wet season, but their roots can’t cope with constantly waterlogged soils. Lone trees separated from the surrounding forest are also susceptible to cyclones, which reach speeds of over 200 kph in the area, and can easily uproot even a 30-metre baobab.</p><p>Cây bao báp thích nghi với mùa mưa hằng năm, nhưng rễ của chúng không thể chịu được tình trạng đất bị ngập úng liên tục. Những cây đơn lẻ, tách khỏi khu rừng xung quanh, cũng dễ bị ảnh hưởng bởi các cơn bão nhiệt đới, có thể đạt tốc độ hơn 200 km/h trong khu vực, và dễ dàng quật đổ ngay cả một cây bao báp cao 30 mét.</p>", "9": "<p><b>9. farming</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>land clearance: mainly by burning carried out to create space for ________ activity</i></p><p>(Phá rừng: chủ yếu bằng cách đốt để tạo không gian cho ________hoạt động)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>David Baum from the University of Wisconsin has spent years researching Malagasy baobabs. ‘The main threat to their survival is land clearance for farming, which probably suppresses regeneration,’ he says.</p><p>David Baum từ Đại học Wisconsin đã dành nhiều năm nghiên cứu về cây bao báp Madagascar. Ông nói: &quot;Mối đe dọa chính đối với sự sống còn của chúng là việc phá rừng để phục vụ nông nghiệp, điều này có thể kìm hãm sự tái sinh.&quot;</p>", "10": "<p><b>10. logging</b></p><p>Câu hỏi:   <i>practice of ______makes seed germination difficult</i></p><p>(Tập tục _______khiến việc nảy mầm của hạt giống trở nên khó khăn)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Human disturbance in the forest, such as logging, is also a problem. This practice may be promoting the rapid spread of shrubby undergrowth, which quickly fills in the bright forest gaps needed for baobab germination.</p><p>Sự can thiệp của con người vào rừng, chẳng hạn như hoạt động khai thác gỗ, cũng là một vấn đề. Thực tiễn này có thể đang thúc đẩy sự lan rộng nhanh chóng của các bụi cây thấp, vốn nhanh chóng lấp đầy những khoảng trống sáng trong rừng – điều cần thiết cho sự nảy mầm của cây bao báp.</p>", "11": "<p><b>11.animals</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>disappearance of certain ________which dispersed baobab seeds</i></p><p>(Sự biến mất của một số _______giúp phát tán hạt giống bao báp)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Baum suggests that dispersal is being limited by the absence of two large, now extinct animals– a gorilla – sized lemur and the three-metre-tall elephant bird. Passage through the digestive tract of these giant fruit-eating animals probably once played a vital role in the distribution of the renala baobabs in particular.</p><p>Baum cho rằng sự phát tán (của loài cây này) đang bị hạn chế do sự biến mất của hai loài động vật lớn từng tồn tại nhưng nay đã tuyệt chủng — một loài vượn cáo to cỡ khỉ đột và loài chim voi cao ba mét. Việc đi qua hệ tiêu hóa của những động vật khổng lồ ăn trái cây này có lẽ từng đóng vai trò quan trọng trong việc phân tán loài bao báp renala, đặc biệt là trước đây.</p>", "12": "<p><b>12. maps</b></p><p>Câu hỏi:  <i>making ________ of areas of relatively undamaged baobab forest</i></p><p>(Lập bản đồ_______ các khu vực rừng bao báp tương đối không bị hư hại)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>On his expeditions Bond works closely with local people to search for, and create maps of baobab distribution.</p><p>Trong các chuyến thám hiểm của mình, Bond làm việc chặt chẽ với người dân địa phương để tìm kiếm và lập bản đồ phân bố của cây bao báp.</p>", "13": "<p><b>13. book</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>producing a ________showing importance of baobab in local culture</i></p><p>(Tạo ra một _______ cho thấy tầm quan trọng của cây bao báp trong văn hóa địa phương)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>He also plans to compile an illustrated children’s book of the many local folk-tales about the tree, hoping that national and international versions will help to spread the word about how important baobabs are in Malagasy culture.</p><p>Ông cũng dự định biên soạn một cuốn sách thiếu nhi có minh họa về nhiều câu chuyện dân gian địa phương liên quan đến cây bao báp, với hy vọng rằng các phiên bản trong nước và quốc tế sẽ giúp lan tỏa thông điệp về tầm quan trọng của loài cây này trong văn hóa Madagascar.</p>", "14": "<p>14. vii</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>There is even a belief that they will one day be obsolete, as will paper or plastic notes, and money will at last have achieved the pure status of a mathematical formulation, a system of electronic credits and debits.</p><p>Thậm chí còn có một niềm tin rằng một ngày nào đó chúng sẽ trở nên lỗi thời, giống như tiền giấy hay tiền nhựa, và tiền tệ cuối cùng sẽ đạt đến trạng thái thuần túy của một công thức toán học – một hệ thống các khoản ghi có và ghi nợ điện tử.</p>", "15": "<p>15. xi</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>This is exactly the way ancient coins have informed us for over four millennia. There are thousands of different coins, each designed to carry meaning as well as value, tiny but rich sources of documentary evidence about events and cultural attitudes, frequently produced with exquisite craftsmanship.</p><p>Đây chính xác là cách mà các đồng tiền cổ đã cung cấp thông tin cho chúng ta trong suốt hơn bốn thiên niên kỷ. Có hàng nghìn loại tiền xu khác nhau, mỗi loại đều được thiết kế không chỉ để mang giá trị mà còn truyền tải ý nghĩa, là những nguồn tư liệu nhỏ bé nhưng phong phú về các sự kiện và thái độ văn hóa, thường được chế tác với tay nghề tinh xảo.</p>", "16": "<p>16. v</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Metal coins were far more portable, and not subject to the same spoilage on long sea journeys as a commodity like grain might be if it became wet.</p><p>Tiền kim loại dễ mang theo hơn nhiều và không bị hư hỏng trong những chuyến đi biển dài như các loại hàng hóa như ngũ cốc có thể bị nếu bị ẩm ướt.</p>", "17": "<p>17. iv</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The value of coins was generally based on the intrinsic value of the metal they were made of.</p><p>Giá trị của tiền xu thường dựa trên giá trị nội tại của kim loại dùng để chế tạo ra chúng.</p>", "18": "<p>18. ii</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The Greek view of life was deeply motivated by a spirit of competition, and coins, which circulated around the trading world, were a way of advertising the qualities of each particular city-state.</p><p>Quan niệm sống của người Hy Lạp chịu ảnh hưởng sâu sắc bởi tinh thần cạnh tranh, và tiền xu – lưu hành khắp thế giới buôn bán – là một cách để quảng bá những đặc điểm, thế mạnh riêng của từng thành bang.</p>", "19": "<p>19. ix</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The beautiful features of the city&#x27;s patron, Athena (with her owl on the reverse of the coin), seem to have been repeated virtually unaltered for many years. Perhaps this was because the Athenian currency had grown increasingly to be the international standard and, like the US dollar today, a uniform design implied integrity.</p><p>Những đường nét tuyệt đẹp của nữ thần bảo hộ thành phố, Athena (với hình con cú ở mặt sau đồng xu), dường như đã được lặp lại gần như không thay đổi trong nhiều năm. Có lẽ điều này là do tiền tệ của Athens ngày càng trở thành tiêu chuẩn quốc tế và, giống như đồng đô la Mỹ ngày nay, một thiết kế thống nhất thể hiện tính toàn vẹn.</p>", "20": "<p>20. vi</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>One side of a coin was always occupied by the head of a divinity, so it was a relatively small step, at around 323 BC, for Greek coins to replace that head with the portrait of the reigning monarch, a tradition that has continued in monarchies to the present day. Republics may have either symbolic heads – going back to the older tradition of divinities – or the features of a distinguished past head of state. The Romans adopted the Greek practice of coin design and as a result, the features of many great Romans have survived and are known to modern generations, despite the fact that the art of portrait painting was completely lost for more than a millennium following the fall of the Roman Empire.</p><p>Một mặt của đồng xu luôn được khắc hình đầu của một vị thần, vì vậy, vào khoảng năm 323 trước Công nguyên, việc Hy Lạp thay thế hình đầu đó bằng chân dung của vị vua trị vì trên đồng xu là một bước tiến tương đối nhỏ, một truyền thống đã được các chế độ quân chủ tiếp tục cho đến ngày nay. Các nước cộng hòa có thể sử dụng hình ảnh tượng trưng – quay trở lại truyền thống cổ xưa về các vị thần – hoặc hình ảnh của một nguyên thủ quốc gia lỗi lạc trong quá khứ. Người La Mã đã tiếp thu cách thiết kế tiền xu của người Hy Lạp và kết quả là, chân dung của nhiều người La Mã vĩ đại đã được lưu giữ và biết đến bởi các thế hệ hiện đại, mặc dù nghệ thuật vẽ chân dung đã hoàn toàn bị thất truyền trong hơn một thiên niên kỷ sau sự sụp đổ của Đế chế La Mã.</p>", "21": "<p><b>21-22. C, A (in any order)</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Which TWO of these comments about ancient coins are made by the writer of the text?</i></p><p>A.    There is no record of the names of the coin makers.B.    Coins were not popular as payment for traded objects.C.    Coins with the same design often looked quite different.D.    Greek coins were more beautiful in design than Roman coins.E.    At times, too many coins were produced in Greece.</p><p>(<i>Hai trong số những nhận xét sau đây về tiền xu cổ được tác giả của văn bản đưa ra?</i>A.    Không có ghi chép nào về tên của những người đúc tiền.B.   Tiền xu không phổ biến như một phương tiện thanh toán cho các vật phẩm được giao dịch.C.    Những đồng tiền có cùng thiết kế thường trông khá khác nhau.D.    Tiền xu Hy Lạp có thiết kế đẹp hơn tiền xu La Mã.E.    Đôi khi, Hy Lạp sản xuất quá nhiều tiền xu.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>This explains the endless variety encountered in coins with the same designs because the moulds had to be remade repeatedly, by many different artisans. One of the distinctive features of coins, in fact, is that they are the work of anonymous craftsmen, and yet each coin is an intimate reflection of the character of its city of origin.</p><p>Điều này giải thích sự đa dạng gần như vô tận của các đồng tiền có cùng thiết kế, bởi vì các khuôn đúc phải được làm lại nhiều lần bởi nhiều nghệ nhân khác nhau. Thực tế, một trong những đặc điểm nổi bật của tiền xu là chúng là sản phẩm của những người thợ vô danh, nhưng mỗi đồng tiền lại phản ánh một cách gần gũi bản sắc của thành phố nơi nó được tạo ra.</p>", "22": "<p><b>21-22. C, A (in any order)</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Which TWO of these comments about ancient coins are made by the writer of the text?</i></p><p>A.    There is no record of the names of the coin makers.B.    Coins were not popular as payment for traded objects.C.    Coins with the same design often looked quite different.D.    Greek coins were more beautiful in design than Roman coins.E.    At times, too many coins were produced in Greece.</p><p>(<i>Hai trong số những nhận xét sau đây về tiền xu cổ được tác giả của văn bản đưa ra?</i>A.    Không có ghi chép nào về tên của những người đúc tiền.B.   Tiền xu không phổ biến như một phương tiện thanh toán cho các vật phẩm được giao dịch.C.    Những đồng tiền có cùng thiết kế thường trông khá khác nhau.D.    Tiền xu Hy Lạp có thiết kế đẹp hơn tiền xu La Mã.E.    Đôi khi, Hy Lạp sản xuất quá nhiều tiền xu.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>This explains the endless variety encountered in coins with the same designs because the moulds had to be remade repeatedly, by many different artisans. One of the distinctive features of coins, in fact, is that they are the work of anonymous craftsmen, and yet each coin is an intimate reflection of the character of its city of origin.</p><p>Điều này giải thích sự đa dạng gần như vô tận của các đồng tiền có cùng thiết kế, bởi vì các khuôn đúc phải được làm lại nhiều lần bởi nhiều nghệ nhân khác nhau. Thực tế, một trong những đặc điểm nổi bật của tiền xu là chúng là sản phẩm của những người thợ vô danh, nhưng mỗi đồng tiền lại phản ánh một cách gần gũi bản sắc của thành phố nơi nó được tạo ra.</p>", "23": "<p><b>23. civilisation</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The ancient Greek _______ was made up of many city-states which were spread over a large area.</i></p><p>(Vương quốc Hy Lạp cổ đại_______bao gồm nhiều thành bang trải rộng trên một khu vực lớn.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Their civilisation, which was composed of dozens of city-states, was highly decentralised.</p><p>Nền văn minh của họ, vốn bao gồm hàng chục thành bang, có mức độ phi tập trung rất cao.</p>", "24": "<p><b>24. barter</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Many Greeks were seafarers who travelled the seas trading. Before coins, they had to do this through______</i></p><p>(Nhiều người Hy Lạp là thủy thủ, họ đi biển buôn bán. Trước khi có tiền xu, họ phải làm điều này thông qua_____)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Despite the potential for theft, the Greeks realised that they could benefit enormously from a system that used coins, because it meant that they no longer had to carry goods with them to barter.</p><p>Mặc dù có nguy cơ bị trộm cắp, người Hy Lạp nhận ra rằng họ có thể hưởng lợi rất lớn từ một hệ thống sử dụng tiền xu, bởi vì điều đó có nghĩa là họ không còn phải mang theo hàng hóa để trao đổi nữa.</p>", "25": "<p><b>25. silver</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The Greeks had a plentiful supply of _______ which they mined themselves</i></p><p>(Người Hy Lạp có nguồn cung cấp_______ dồi dào mà họ tự khai thác,)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The value of coins was generally based on the intrinsic value of the metal they were made of. At first this was mostly silver, and this meant that the discovery of silver mines on Greek territory actually increased the quantity of money they could produce.</p><p>Giá trị của tiền xu thường dựa trên giá trị nội tại của kim loại dùng để chế tạo ra chúng. Ban đầu, kim loại này chủ yếu là bạc, và điều đó có nghĩa là việc phát hiện ra các mỏ bạc trên lãnh thổ Hy Lạp thực sự đã làm tăng lượng tiền mà họ có thể sản xuất.</p>", "26": "<p><b>26. inflation</b></p><p>Câu hỏi:<i> but were able to avoid the problem of ______-which the Spanish encountered much later, after they allowed too much gold from the New World to enter their economy.</i></p><p>(nhưng họ đã tránh được vấn đề ______mà người Tây Ban Nha gặp phải sau này, sau khi họ cho phép quá nhiều vàng từ Tân Thế giới xâm nhập vào nền kinh tế của mình.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Had the Greek mining been on a larger scale, it could have caused problems similar to those faced by the Spanish centuries later, when so much gold from the New World was brought into their economy that it resulted in serious inflation.</p><p>Nếu hoạt động khai thác vàng của người Hy Lạp diễn ra trên quy mô lớn hơn, nó có thể gây ra những vấn đề tương tự như những vấn đề mà người Tây Ban Nha phải đối mặt nhiều thế kỷ sau đó, khi quá nhiều vàng từ Tân Thế giới được đưa vào nền kinh tế của họ dẫn đến lạm phát nghiêm trọng.</p>", "27": "<p><b>27. B</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>details of an experiment in taste comparison</i></p><p>(Chi tiết về một thí nghiệm so sánh vị giác)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>In a test conducted by a biotechnology company, sets of two miniature white paper cups, labelled 304 and 305, were placed before five people seated around a conference table.</p><p>Trong một thí nghiệm do một công ty công nghệ sinh học thực hiện, các bộ gồm hai cốc giấy trắng nhỏ, được dán nhãn 304 và 305, đã được đặt trước năm người ngồi quanh một bàn họp.</p>", "28": "<p><b>28. H</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>examples of medications that could be improved using compounds that remove bitterness</i></p><p>(Ví dụ về các loại thuốc có thể được cải thiện bằng cách sử dụng các hợp chất loại bỏ vị đắng)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Bitter blockers could also be used in place of sugary cherry and grape flavourings in children&#x27;s cough syrups, and they could dampen the bitterness of drugs like antihistamines, antibiotics and other similar products.</p><p>Các chất chặn vị đắng cũng có thể được sử dụng thay cho hương vị anh đào và nho có đường trong siro ho dành cho trẻ em, và chúng có thể làm giảm vị đắng của các loại thuốc như thuốc kháng histamin, kháng sinh và các sản phẩm tương tự khác.</p>", "29": "<p><b>29. E</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>a biological reason why some people don&#x27;t like bitter-tasting foods</i></p><p>(Lý do sinh học tại sao một số người không thích thức ăn có vị đắng)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>About 75% of people are sensitive to the taste of some well-identified bitter compounds. Those who are sensitive seem to be less likely than others to eat bitter vegetables, according to Stephen Wooding, a geneticist at the University of Utah.</p><p>Khoảng 75% người nhạy cảm với vị của một số hợp chất đắng đã được xác định rõ. Theo Stephen Wooding, một nhà di truyền học tại Đại học Utah, những người nhạy cảm dường như ít có khả năng ăn rau đắng hơn những người khác.</p>", "30": "<p><b>30.F</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>an explanation of how the taste process works</i></p><p>(một lời giải thích về cách quá trình vị giác hoạt động)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The G protein involved in the perception of bitterness, sweetness and umami was identified in the early 1990s by Robert Margolskee, at Mount Sinai School of Medicine in New York. The protein triggers a cascade of chemical reactions that lead to ion concentrations within the cell. Ultimately, this delivers a signal to the brain that registers as bitter.</p><p>Protein G, tham gia vào quá trình cảm nhận vị đắng, vị ngọt và vị umami, được Robert Margolskee xác định vào đầu những năm 1990 tại Trường Y Mount Sinai ở New York. Protein này kích hoạt một chuỗi phản ứng hóa học dẫn đến sự thay đổi nồng độ ion bên trong tế bào. Cuối cùng, điều này truyền tín hiệu đến não bộ và nhận biết vị đắng.</p>", "31": "<p><b>31. C</b></p><p>Câu hỏi:  <i>why bitterness is more interesting commercially than other tastes</i></p><p>(tại sao vị đắng lại hấp dẫn về mặt thương mại hơn các vị khác)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>However, bitterness is of special interest to the food industry because it is present in so many different types of food.</p><p>Tuy nhiên, vị đắng đặc biệt được ngành công nghiệp thực phẩm quan tâm vì nó xuất hiện trong rất nhiều loại thực phẩm khác nhau</p>", "32": "<p><b>32. H</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>an example of how compounds that remove bitterness could indirectly benefit health</i></p><p>(Một ví dụ về cách các hợp chất loại bỏ vị đắng có thể gián tiếp mang lại lợi ích cho sức khỏe)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Consider, for example, that a single cup of canned chicken noodle soup contains 850 milligrams of sodium chloride, or table salt - more than a third of the recommended daily allowance. The salt masks the bitterness created by the high temperatures used in the canning process, which cause sugars and amino acids to react. Part of the table salt could be replaced by another type of salt, potassium chloride, which tends to be scarce in some people&#x27;s diets because of its bitter taste. But that bitter taste could be eliminated with a dose of AMP.</p><p>Hãy xem xét, ví dụ, một cốc súp gà mì đóng hộp chứa khoảng 850 miligam natri clorua (tức muối ăn) – nhiều hơn một phần ba lượng khuyến nghị tiêu thụ hằng ngày. Muối này giúp che đi vị đắng sinh ra bởi nhiệt độ cao trong quá trình đóng hộp, khi đường và axit amin phản ứng với nhau.Một phần muối ăn có thể được thay thế bằng một loại muối khác là kali clorua, vốn thường thiếu trong chế độ ăn của một số người do có vị đắng. Tuy nhiên, vị đắng đó có thể được loại bỏ bằng cách sử dụng một lượng AMP.</p>", "33": "<p><b>33. D</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>a reason why people have different taste preferences from animals</i></p><p>(Lý do tại sao con người có sở thích vị giác khác với động vật)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>&#x27;Every other species is averse to bitter because it means bad news. But we have learned to enjoy it.&#x27;</p><p>“Mọi loài khác đều tránh vị đắng vì nó báo hiệu điều không tốt. Nhưng con người chúng ta đã học cách thưởng thức nó.”</p>", "34": "<p><b>34. naringin</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The grapefruit used to make drinks is chosen because it contains a smaller amount of a substance called_______</i></p><p>(Bưởi dùng để pha đồ uống được chọn vì nó chứa một lượng nhỏ chất gọi là_____)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Most people don&#x27;t want to drink more than a few ounces at a time. This is because a natural chemical compound found in grapefruit, naringin, has a bitter taste.</p><p>Hầu hết mọi người không muốn uống quá vài ounce mỗi lần. Điều này là do một hợp chất hóa học tự nhiên có trong bưởi, gọi là naringin, có vị đắng.</p>", "35": "<p><b>35. poisonous</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Animals associate a bitter taste with ______plants.</i></p><p>(Động vật liên kết vị đắng với _______ thực vật.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>They defend plants by warning animals away and protect animals by letting them know when a plant may be poisonous.</p><p>Chúng bảo vệ thực vật bằng cách cảnh báo động vật tránh xa và bảo vệ động vật bằng cách cho chúng biết khi nào một loại cây có thể gây độc.</p>", "36": "<p><b>36. genetic</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Our varying degree of sensitivity to bitter tastes is thought to be______</i></p><p>(Mức độ nhạy cảm khác nhau của chúng ta đối với vị đắng được cho là_______)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>People have varying capacities for tasting bitterness and the differences appear to be genetic.</p><p>Khả năng cảm nhận vị đắng của mỗi người là khác nhau và sự khác biệt này dường như có liên quan đến yếu tố di truyền.</p>", "37": "<p><b>37. supertasters</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>People who are extremely aware of bitter tastes are called _______</i></p><p>(Những người cực kỳ nhạy cảm với vị đắng được gọi là_______)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>Some people who have an unusually high number of taste buds, and are known as &#x27;supertasters&#x27;, are especially sensitive to these compounds.</p><p>Một số người có số lượng nụ vị giác nhiều bất thường, được gọi là &quot;siêu vị giác&quot;, đặc biệt nhạy cảm với các hợp chất này.</p>", "38": "<p><b>38. taste buds</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>Receptors inside the ________ on the tongue detect the taste of food and drink.</i></p><p>(Các thụ thể bên trong ________ trên lưỡi phát hiện vị của thức ăn và đồ uống.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The tongue is covered with taste buds. Under the microscope each one looks like an onion, consisting of 50 to 100 elongated cells running from the top of the taste buds to the bottom. At the top is a group of receptors that captures the taste molecules, known as tastants, in food and drink.</p><p>Lưỡi được bao phủ bởi các gai vị giác. Dưới kính hiển vi mỗi gai nhìn giống như 1 củ hành, bao gồm 50 đến 100 tế bào hình chai chạy từ đỉnh tới chân gai vị giác. Ở đỉnh là 1 nhóm thụ thể có chức năng thu nhận các phân tử mùi vị, còn gọi là chất tạo vị, trong thức ăn và đồ uống</p>", "39": "<p><b>39. A</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>The G protein identified by Robert Margolskee</i>A is responsible for transmitting bitter tastes.B causes dangerous reactions in the cells of the tongue. C has a similar function to sight and smell receptors.D can be found only in sweetness and umami.</p><p>(<i>Protein G được Robert Margolskee xác định</i>A chịu trách nhiệm truyền tải vị đắng.B gây ra các phản ứng nguy hiểm trong các tế bào của lưỡi.C có chức năng tương tự như các thụ thể thị giác và khứu giác.D chỉ có thể được tìm thấy trong vị ngọt và vị umami.)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>The protein triggers a cascade of chemical reactions that lead to ion concentrations within the cell</p><p>Loại protein này kích hoạt một chuỗi phản ứng hóa học dẫn đến sự thay đổi nồng độ ion bên trong tế bào.</p>", "40": "<p><b>40. C</b></p><p>Câu hỏi: <i>AMP is an organic compound which is</i>A found only in breast milk.B bitter-tasting when added to foodC able to reduce some bitter tastesD found in bitter taste receptors</p><p>(<i>AMP là một hợp chất hữu cơ</i>A chỉ được tìm thấy trong sữa mẹ.B có vị đắng khi thêm vào thức ănC có khả năng làm giảm một số vị đắngD được tìm thấy trong các thụ thể vị đắng)</p><p><u><i><b>Đoạn chứa đáp án</b></i></u>:</p><p>AMP has no bitterness of its own, but when put in foods, as Margolskee and his colleagues discovered, it attaches to bitter-taste receptors</p><p>AMP bản thân nó không có vị đắng, nhưng khi được đưa vào thực phẩm, như Margolskee và các đồng nghiệp đã phát hiện, nó sẽ gắn vào các thụ thể vị đắng.</p>"}, "transcriptSegments": {"1": [{"id": "r1-p1", "text": "The island of Madagascar in the Indian Ocean is the best place in the world to see baobabs. These spectacular trees, with their massive trunks and distinctiye horizontal root-like branches, can live over 800 years. Of the eight known species, six are found only there, while a seventh has made its way across the Mozambique Channel from mainland Africa with a certain amount of human help. Some of the most visited and photographed baobabs in Madagascar are at the Avenue des Baobabs in the western province of Menabe, close to the town of Morondava. There, a dozen trees straddle a narrow sandy road, soaring 30 metres into the sky. Known as renala – ‘mother of the forest’ - in the local Malagasy* language, these trees belong to the tallest baobab species in the world: Adansonia grandidieri, named after two great 19th-century French botanists, Michel Adanson and Alfred Grandidier.", "html": "<p>The island of Madagascar in the Indian Ocean is the best place in the world to see baobabs. These spectacular trees, with their massive trunks and distinctiye horizontal root-like branches, can live over 800 years. Of the eight known species, six are found only there, while a seventh has made its way across the Mozambique Channel from mainland Africa with a certain amount of human help. Some of the most visited and photographed baobabs in Madagascar are at the Avenue des Baobabs in the western province of Menabe, close to the town of Morondava. There, a dozen trees straddle a narrow sandy road, soaring 30 metres into the sky. Known as renala – ‘mother of the forest’ - in the local <i>Malagasy*</i> language, these trees belong to the tallest baobab species in the world: Adansonia grandidieri, named after two great 19th-century French botanists, Michel Adanson and Alfred Grandidier.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r1-p2", "text": "Baobabs have religious and spiritual significance for many Malagasy tribes, although the majority of the island’s population will probably never see one – they only grow along the western margin of the island and not in the more populous central highlands. However, as the national tree and the national emblem, the trees are an ideal symbol for increasing awareness not only of the endangered forests, but also of the traditional beliefs of the people who live alongside them. But what does the future hold for the Malagasy baobabs?", "html": "<p>Baobabs have religious and spiritual significance for many Malagasy tribes, although the majority of the island’s population will probably never see one – they only grow along the western margin of the island and not in the more populous central highlands. However, as the national tree and the national emblem, the trees are an ideal symbol for increasing awareness not only of the endangered forests, but also of the traditional beliefs of the people who live alongside them. But what does the future hold for the Malagasy baobabs?</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r1-p3", "text": "The Avenue des Baobabs is something of a contradiction; the ‘natural’ scene that tourists visit is actually man-made. While the trees themselves are natural enough, the surrounding landscape was created by humans. Back in the early 20th century, much of the Morondava plain was cleared for rice and sugar-cane fields. However, many baobabs were spared because they are valuable as a food source and renewable building material, or they simply survived the fires that were set to clear the dry deciduous forest around them. These days many stand alone in fields, a reminder of where the forest once was. But now it seems that even the mighty baobabs are falling down. ‘Every year I see new trees that have fallen,’ says Jim Bond, an ethnobotanist who has studied Madagascar’s baobabs for more than a decade. The exact cause of the falling baobabs is uncertain, but some people think that the water used to irrigate the nearby fields is partly to blame. Baobabs are adapted to the annual wet season, but their roots can’t cope with constantly waterlogged soils. Lone trees separated from the surrounding forest are also susceptible to cyclones, which reach speeds of over 200 kph in the area, and can easily uproot even a 30-metre baobab.", "html": "<p>The Avenue des Baobabs is something of a contradiction; the ‘natural’ scene that tourists visit is actually man-made. While the trees themselves are natural enough, the surrounding landscape was created by humans. Back in the early 20th century, much of the Morondava plain was cleared for rice and sugar-cane fields. However, many baobabs were spared because they are valuable as a food source and renewable building material, or they simply survived the fires that were set to clear the dry deciduous forest around them. These days many stand alone in fields, a reminder of where the forest once was. But now it seems that even the mighty baobabs are falling down. ‘Every year I see new trees that have fallen,’ says Jim Bond, an ethnobotanist who has studied Madagascar’s baobabs for more than a decade. The exact cause of the falling baobabs is uncertain, but some people think that the water used to irrigate the nearby fields is partly to blame. Baobabs are adapted to the annual wet season, but their roots can’t cope with constantly waterlogged soils. Lone trees separated from the surrounding forest are also susceptible to cyclones, which reach speeds of over 200 kph in the area, and can easily uproot even a 30-metre baobab.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r1-p4", "text": "Baobabs elsewhere on the island are also facing a host of threats, with three of the six native species now listed as endangered by the World Conservation Union. David Baum from the University of Wisconsin has spent years researching Malagasy baobabs. ‘The main threat to their survival is land clearance for farming, which probably suppresses regeneration,’ he says. Burning is the usual method of clearing land, and while baobabs have some resistance to this, they will eventually succumb to repeated burning. Human disturbance in the forest, such as logging, is also a problem. This practice may be promoting the rapid spread of shrubby undergrowth, which quickly fills in the bright forest gaps needed for baobab germination. Another problem the trees face is the dispersal of their seeds. Baum suggests that dispersal is being limited by the absence of two large, now extinct animals– a gorilla – sized lemur and the three-metre-tall elephant bird. Passage through the digestive tract of these giant fruit-eating animals probably once played a vital role in the distribution of the renala baobabs in particular.", "html": "<p>Baobabs elsewhere on the island are also facing a host of threats, with three of the six native species now listed as endangered by the World Conservation Union. David Baum from the University of Wisconsin has spent years researching Malagasy baobabs. ‘The main threat to their survival is land clearance for farming, which probably suppresses regeneration,’ he says. Burning is the usual method of clearing land, and while baobabs have some resistance to this, they will eventually succumb to repeated burning. Human disturbance in the forest, such as logging, is also a problem. This practice may be promoting the rapid spread of shrubby undergrowth, which quickly fills in the bright forest gaps needed for baobab germination. Another problem the trees face is the dispersal of their seeds. Baum suggests that dispersal is being limited by the absence of two large, now extinct animals– a gorilla – sized lemur and the three-metre-tall elephant bird. Passage through the digestive tract of these giant fruit-eating animals probably once played a vital role in the distribution of the renala baobabs in particular.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r1-p5", "text": "The Malagasy government is becoming increasingly concerned as the baobab is key to the local tourism industry, and thus to the economy of the island as a whole. But visiting and photographing the solitary trees in the dramatic open landscape at the Avenue des Baobabs isn’t the only way to experience baobabs in Madagascar. Within a few hours' drive of Morondava, three species of baobab can be seen in largely intact, dry forest habitat. Jim Bond has led expeditions of Malagasy and foreign participants to the Mikea forest in the island’s southwest, which is almost certainly one of the least studied and least damaged areas of baobab forest left in Madagascar. On his expeditions Bond works closely with local people to search for, and create maps of baobab distribution. He also plans to compile an illustrated children’s book of the many local folk-tales about the tree, hoping that national and international versions will help to spread the word about how important baobabs are in Malagasy culture. But it’s going to require some swift and coordinated action to ensure that the baobab becomes a symbol of conservation success, rather than a symbol of humanity’s mistakes.", "html": "<p>The Malagasy government is becoming increasingly concerned as the baobab is key to the local tourism industry, and thus to the economy of the island as a whole. But visiting and photographing the solitary trees in the dramatic open landscape at the Avenue des Baobabs isn’t the only way to experience baobabs in Madagascar. Within a few hours&#x27; drive of Morondava, three species of baobab can be seen in largely intact, dry forest habitat. Jim Bond has led expeditions of Malagasy and foreign participants to the Mikea forest in the island’s southwest, which is almost certainly one of the least studied and least damaged areas of baobab forest left in Madagascar. On his expeditions Bond works closely with local people to search for, and create maps of baobab distribution. He also plans to compile an illustrated children’s book of the many local folk-tales about the tree, hoping that national and international versions will help to spread the word about how important baobabs are in Malagasy culture. But it’s going to require some swift and coordinated action to ensure that the baobab becomes a symbol of conservation success, rather than a symbol of humanity’s mistakes.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r1-p6", "text": "* Malagasy: of Madagascar", "html": "<p><i>* Malagasy: of Madagascar</i></p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": null}], "2": [{"id": "r2-p1", "text": "Few people pay much attention to coins; we take them for granted. Since the advent of paper currency and the abandonment of a monetary system based on intrinsic of actual value, they have been demoted to the status of change: what is left when you have paid for something with 'paper' money. There is even a belief that they will one day be obsolete, as will paper or plastic notes, and money will at last have achieved the pure status of a mathematical formulation, a system of electronic credits and debits.", "html": "<p>Few people pay much attention to coins; we take them for granted. Since the advent of paper currency and the abandonment of a monetary system based on intrinsic of actual value, they have been demoted to the status of change: what is left when you have paid for something with &#x27;paper&#x27; money. There is even a belief that they will one day be obsolete, as will paper or plastic notes, and money will at last have achieved the pure status of a mathematical formulation, a system of electronic credits and debits.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 14}, {"id": "r2-p2", "text": "In the modern media environment, the sheer number of visual images is overwhelming, but it makes one stop to think that coins are among the most durable of our artefacts, and future archaeologists will scrutinise coins in order to learn about their distant ancestors. This is exactly the way ancient coins have informed us for over four millennia. There are thousands of different coins, each designed to carry meaning as well as value, tiny but rich sources of documentary evidence about events and cultural attitudes, frequently produced with exquisite craftsmanship.", "html": "<p>In the modern media environment, the sheer number of visual images is overwhelming, but it makes one stop to think that coins are among the most durable of our artefacts, and future archaeologists will scrutinise coins in order to learn about their distant ancestors. This is exactly the way ancient coins have informed us for over four millennia. There are thousands of different coins, each designed to carry meaning as well as value, tiny but rich sources of documentary evidence about events and cultural attitudes, frequently produced with exquisite craftsmanship.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 15}, {"id": "r2-p3", "text": "Coins in Europe date from 2,500 to 2,700 years ago, and seem to have originated in the ancient kingdom of Lydia, in what is now Turkey. Before coins, trade was based on barter: the direct trade of commodities. Barter required the physical presence of both commodities that were being traded. Metal coins were far more portable, and not subject to the same spoilage on long sea journeys as a commodity like grain might be if it became wet. However, they could also be accumulated, and these hoards could be stolen. Coins were therefore a temptation to criminals. The Greeks, however, were the first to realise the full potential of the new invention. They were a trading people of seafarers. Their civilisation, which was composed of dozens of city-states, was highly decentralised. Despite the potential for theft, the Greeks realised that they could benefit enormously from a system that used coins, because it meant that they no longer had to carry goods with them to barter.", "html": "<p>Coins in Europe date from 2,500 to 2,700 years ago, and seem to have originated in the ancient kingdom of Lydia, in what is now Turkey. Before coins, trade was based on barter: the direct trade of commodities. Barter required the physical presence of both commodities that were being traded. Metal coins were far more portable, and not subject to the same spoilage on long sea journeys as a commodity like grain might be if it became wet. However, they could also be accumulated, and these hoards could be stolen. Coins were therefore a temptation to criminals. The Greeks, however, were the first to realise the full potential of the new invention. They were a trading people of seafarers. Their civilisation, which was composed of dozens of city-states, was highly decentralised. Despite the potential for theft, the Greeks realised that they could benefit enormously from a system that used coins, because it meant that they no longer had to carry goods with them to barter.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 16}, {"id": "r2-p4", "text": "The value of coins was generally based on the intrinsic value of the metal they were made of. At first this was mostly silver, and this meant that the discovery of silver mines on Greek territory actually increased the quantity of money they could produce. Had the Greek mining been on a larger scale, it could have caused problems similar to those faced by the Spanish centuries later, when so much gold from the New World was brought into their economy that it resulted in serious inflation. One advantage of intrinsic value was that coins could be weighed to determine the amount of metal they contained, so that the only thing an issuing state had to guarantee was the purity of the metal to prevent accusations of fraud and cheating, which could cause fighting and unrest. This was quite difficult to do, especially with coins made of gold, until Archimedes realised that different metals have different weight-volume ratios, and that by measuring the amount of displacement when coins were immersed in water, it was possible to establish whether gold was alloyed with silver.", "html": "<p>The value of coins was generally based on the intrinsic value of the metal they were made of. At first this was mostly silver, and this meant that the discovery of silver mines on Greek territory actually increased the quantity of money they could produce. Had the Greek mining been on a larger scale, it could have caused problems similar to those faced by the Spanish centuries later, when so much gold from the New World was brought into their economy that it resulted in serious inflation. One advantage of intrinsic value was that coins could be weighed to determine the amount of metal they contained, so that the only thing an issuing state had to guarantee was the purity of the metal to prevent accusations of fraud and cheating, which could cause fighting and unrest. This was quite difficult to do, especially with coins made of gold, until Archimedes realised that different metals have different weight-volume ratios, and that by measuring the amount of displacement when coins were immersed in water, it was possible to establish whether gold was alloyed with silver.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 17}, {"id": "r2-p5", "text": "All ancient coins were made by hand using engraved metal moulds. Many hundreds of coins were made, and as a result the moulds often cracked and new ones had to be engraved. This explains the endless variety encountered in coins with the same designs because the moulds had to be remade repeatedly, by many different artisans. One of the distinctive features of coins, in fact, is that they are the work of anonymous craftsmen, and yet each coin is an intimate reflection of the character of its city of origin. It was the Greeks who realised that coins could have a double function, as a unit of value and as a symbol of the identity of the city that issued them. The Greek view of life was deeply motivated by a spirit of competition, and coins, which circulated around the trading world, were a way of advertising the qualities of each particular city-state.", "html": "<p>All ancient coins were made by hand using engraved metal moulds. Many hundreds of coins were made, and as a result the moulds often cracked and new ones had to be engraved. This explains the endless variety encountered in coins with the same designs because the moulds had to be remade repeatedly, by many different artisans. One of the distinctive features of coins, in fact, is that they are the work of anonymous craftsmen, and yet each coin is an intimate reflection of the character of its city of origin. It was the Greeks who realised that coins could have a double function, as a unit of value and as a symbol of the identity of the city that issued them. The Greek view of life was deeply motivated by a spirit of competition, and coins, which circulated around the trading world, were a way of advertising the qualities of each particular city-state.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 18}, {"id": "r2-p6", "text": "Interestingly, Greek coins from the city of Athens, which was the home of the greatest sculptors of the classical period, seem to have been completely disconnected from the high sculptural tradition. The beautiful features of the city's patron, Athena (with her owl on the reverse of the coin), seem to have been repeated virtually unaltered for many years. Perhaps this was because the Athenian currency had grown increasingly to be the international standard and, like the US dollar today, a uniform design implied integrity.", "html": "<p>Interestingly, Greek coins from the city of Athens, which was the home of the greatest sculptors of the classical period, seem to have been completely disconnected from the high sculptural tradition. The beautiful features of the city&#x27;s patron, Athena (with her owl on the reverse of the coin), seem to have been repeated virtually unaltered for many years. Perhaps this was because the Athenian currency had grown increasingly to be the international standard and, like the US dollar today, a uniform design implied integrity.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 19}, {"id": "r2-p7", "text": "One side of a coin was always occupied by the head of a divinity, so it was a relatively small step, at around 323 BC, for Greek coins to replace that head with the portrait of the reigning monarch, a tradition that has continued in monarchies to the present day. Republics may have either symbolic heads – going back to the older tradition of divinities – or the features of a distinguished past head of state. The Romans adopted the Greek practice of coin design and as a result, the features of many great Romans have survived and are known to modern generations, despite the fact that the art of portrait painting was completely lost for more than a millennium following the fall of the Roman Empire.", "html": "<p>One side of a coin was always occupied by the head of a divinity, so it was a relatively small step, at around 323 BC, for Greek coins to replace that head with the portrait of the reigning monarch, a tradition that has continued in monarchies to the present day. Republics may have either symbolic heads – going back to the older tradition of divinities – or the features of a distinguished past head of state. The Romans adopted the Greek practice of coin design and as a result, the features of many great Romans have survived and are known to modern generations, despite the fact that the art of portrait painting was completely lost for more than a millennium following the fall of the Roman Empire.</p>", "letter": null, "headingQuestion": 20}], "3": [{"id": "r3-pA", "text": "A There is a reason why grapefruit juice is served in little glasses. Most people don't want to drink more than a few ounces at a time. This is because a natural chemical compound found in grapefruit, naringin, has a bitter taste. Some people like that bitterness in small doses and believe it enhances the general flavour, but others would rather avoid it altogether. So juice packagers often select grapefruit with low naringin content, even though the compound has antioxidant properties that some nutritionists contend may help prevent many serious diseases.", "html": "<p><b>A </b>There is a reason why grapefruit juice is served in little glasses. Most people don&#x27;t want to drink more than a few ounces at a time. This is because a natural chemical compound found in grapefruit, naringin, has a bitter taste. Some people like that bitterness in small doses and believe it enhances the general flavour, but others would rather avoid it altogether. So juice packagers often select grapefruit with low naringin content, even though the compound has antioxidant properties that some nutritionists contend may help prevent many serious diseases.</p>", "letter": "A", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pB", "text": "B It is possible, however, to get the goodness of grapefruit without the bitter taste. In a test conducted by a biotechnology company, sets of two miniature white paper cups, labelled 304 and 305, were placed before five people seated around a conference table. Each person drank from one cup and then the other, cleansing their palates between tastes with water and a cracker. Even the smallest sip of 304 had grapefruit's unmistakeable bite. But 305 was smoother; there was the sour taste of citrus but none of the bitterness of naringin. This juice had been treated with AMP, a compound that blocks the bitterness in foods without making them less nutritious.", "html": "<p><b>B</b> It is possible, however, to get the goodness of grapefruit without the bitter taste. In a test conducted by a biotechnology company, sets of two miniature white paper cups, labelled 304 and 305, were placed before five people seated around a conference table. Each person drank from one cup and then the other, cleansing their palates between tastes with water and a cracker. Even the smallest sip of 304 had grapefruit&#x27;s unmistakeable bite. But 305 was smoother; there was the sour taste of citrus but none of the bitterness of naringin. This juice had been treated with AMP, a compound that blocks the bitterness in foods without making them less nutritious.</p>", "letter": "B", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pC", "text": "C Taste research is a booming business these days, with scientists delving into all five basics: sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami (the savory taste of protein). However, bitterness is of special interest to the food industry because it is present in so many different types of food. There are thousands of bitter-tasting compounds in nature. They defend plants by warning animals away and protect animals by letting them know when a plant may be poisonous. But the system isn't foolproof. Grapefruit and bitter green vegetables like Brussels sprouts and kale are nutritious despite - and sometimes because of - their bitter-tasting components.", "html": "<p><b>C </b>Taste research is a booming business these days, with scientists delving into all five basics: sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami (the savory taste of protein). However, bitterness is of special interest to the food industry because it is present in so many different types of food. There are thousands of bitter-tasting compounds in nature. They defend plants by warning animals away and protect animals by letting them know when a plant may be poisonous. But the system isn&#x27;t foolproof. Grapefruit and bitter green vegetables like Brussels sprouts and kale are nutritious despite - and sometimes because of - their bitter-tasting components.</p>", "letter": "C", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pD", "text": "D 'Humans are the only species that enjoys a bitter taste,' says Charles Zuker, a neuroscientist at the University of California School of Medicine at San Diego. 'Every other species is averse to bitter because it means bad news. But we have learned to enjoy it. We drink coffee, which is bitter, and quinine (in tonic water) too. We enjoy having that spice in our lives.' Because bitterness can be pleasant in small quantities but repellent when intense, bitter blockers like AMP could make a whole range of foods, drinks and medicines more palatable.", "html": "<p><b>D</b> &#x27;Humans are the only species that enjoys a bitter taste,&#x27; says Charles Zuker, a neuroscientist at the University of California School of Medicine at San Diego. &#x27;Every other species is averse to bitter because it means bad news. But we have learned to enjoy it. We drink coffee, which is bitter, and quinine (in tonic water) too. We enjoy having that spice in our lives.&#x27; Because bitterness can be pleasant in small quantities but repellent when intense, bitter blockers like AMP could make a whole range of foods, drinks and medicines more palatable.</p>", "letter": "D", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pE", "text": "E People have varying capacities for tasting bitterness and the differences appear to be genetic. About 75% of people are sensitive to the taste of some well-identified bitter compounds. Those who are sensitive seem to be less likely than others to eat bitter vegetables, according to Stephen Wooding, a geneticist at the University of Utah. Some people who have an unusually high number of taste buds, and are known as 'supertasters', are especially sensitive to these compounds. Supertasters tend to shun all kinds of bitter-tasting things, including vegetables, coffee, and dark chocolate.", "html": "<p><b>E </b>People have varying capacities for tasting bitterness and the differences appear to be genetic. About 75% of people are sensitive to the taste of some well-identified bitter compounds. Those who are sensitive seem to be less likely than others to eat bitter vegetables, according to Stephen Wooding, a geneticist at the University of Utah. Some people who have an unusually high number of taste buds, and are known as &#x27;supertasters&#x27;, are especially sensitive to these compounds. Supertasters tend to shun all kinds of bitter-tasting things, including vegetables, coffee, and dark chocolate.</p>", "letter": "E", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pF", "text": "F The tongue is covered with taste buds. Under the microscope each one looks like an onion, consisting of 50 to 100 elongated cells running from the top of the taste buds to the bottom. At the top is a group of receptors that captures the taste molecules, known as tastants, in food and drink. The receptors function much like those for sight and smell. Once a bitter signal has been received, it is relayed via proteins known as G proteins. The G protein involved in the perception of bitterness, sweetness and umami was identified in the early 1990s by Robert Margolskee, at Mount Sinai School of Medicine in New York. The protein triggers a cascade of chemical reactions that lead to ion concentrations within the cell. Ultimately, this delivers a signal to the brain that registers as bitter.", "html": "<p><b>F </b>The tongue is covered with taste buds. Under the microscope each one looks like an onion, consisting of 50 to 100 elongated cells running from the top of the taste buds to the bottom. At the top is a group of receptors that captures the taste molecules, known as tastants, in food and drink. The receptors function much like those for sight and smell. Once a bitter signal has been received, it is relayed via proteins known as G proteins. The G protein involved in the perception of bitterness, sweetness and umami was identified in the early 1990s by Robert Margolskee, at Mount Sinai School of Medicine in New York. The protein triggers a cascade of chemical reactions that lead to ion concentrations within the cell. Ultimately, this delivers a signal to the brain that registers as bitter.</p>", "letter": "F", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pG", "text": "G Once they understood the taste mechanism, scientists began to think of ways to interfere with it. They tried AMP, an organic compound found in breast milk and other substances, that is created as cells break down food. AMP has no bitterness of its own, but when put in foods, as Margolskee and his colleagues discovered, it attaches to bitter-taste receptors. As effective as it is, AMP may not be able to dampen every type of bitter taste because it probably doesn't attach to all 30 bitter-taste receptors.", "html": "<p><b>G </b>Once they understood the taste mechanism, scientists began to think of ways to interfere with it. They tried AMP, an organic compound found in breast milk and other substances, that is created as cells break down food. AMP has no bitterness of its own, but when put in foods, as Margolskee and his colleagues discovered, it attaches to bitter-taste receptors. As effective as it is, AMP may not be able to dampen every type of bitter taste because it probably doesn&#x27;t attach to all 30 bitter-taste receptors.</p>", "letter": "G", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pH", "text": "H Some taste researchers believe that, in time, compounds like AMP will help make processed foods less unhealthy. Consider, for example, that a single cup of canned chicken noodle soup contains 850 milligrams of sodium chloride, or table salt - more than a third of the recommended daily allowance. The salt masks the bitterness created by the high temperatures used in the canning process, which cause sugars and amino acids to react. Part of the table salt could be replaced by another type of salt, potassium chloride, which tends to be scarce in some people's diets because of its bitter taste. But that bitter taste could be eliminated with a dose of AMP. Bitter blockers could also be used in place of sugary cherry and grape flavourings in children's cough syrups, and they could dampen the bitterness of drugs like antihistamines, antibiotics and other similar products.", "html": "<p><b>H </b>Some taste researchers believe that, in time, compounds like AMP will help make processed foods less unhealthy. Consider, for example, that a single cup of canned chicken noodle soup contains 850 milligrams of sodium chloride, or table salt - more than a third of the recommended daily allowance. The salt masks the bitterness created by the high temperatures used in the canning process, which cause sugars and amino acids to react. Part of the table salt could be replaced by another type of salt, potassium chloride, which tends to be scarce in some people&#x27;s diets because of its bitter taste. But that bitter taste could be eliminated with a dose of AMP. Bitter blockers could also be used in place of sugary cherry and grape flavourings in children&#x27;s cough syrups, and they could dampen the bitterness of drugs like antihistamines, antibiotics and other similar products.</p>", "letter": "H", "headingQuestion": null}, {"id": "r3-pI", "text": "I A number of foodmakers have already begun to experiment with AMP in their products, and other bitter blockers are being developed. In a few years, perhaps, after food companies have taken the bitterness from canned soup and improved the taste of medicines, they can set their sights on something more useful: a bitter blocker in a bottle that any of us can sprinkle on our Brussels sprouts or stir into our grapefruit juice.", "html": "<p><b>I </b>A number of foodmakers have already begun to experiment with AMP in their products, and other bitter blockers are being developed. In a few years, perhaps, after food companies have taken the bitterness from canned soup and improved the taste of medicines, they can set their sights on something more useful: a bitter blocker in a bottle that any of us can sprinkle on our Brussels sprouts or stir into our grapefruit juice.</p>", "letter": "I", "headingQuestion": null}]}, "transcripts": {"1": "<p>The island of Madagascar in the Indian Ocean is the best place in the world to see baobabs. These spectacular trees, with their massive trunks and distinctiye horizontal root-like branches, can live over 800 years. Of the eight known species, six are found only there, while a seventh has made its way across the Mozambique Channel from mainland Africa with a certain amount of human help. Some of the most visited and photographed baobabs in Madagascar are at the Avenue des Baobabs in the western province of Menabe, close to the town of Morondava. There, a dozen trees straddle a narrow sandy road, soaring 30 metres into the sky. Known as renala – ‘mother of the forest’ - in the local <i>Malagasy*</i> language, these trees belong to the tallest baobab species in the world: Adansonia grandidieri, named after two great 19th-century French botanists, Michel Adanson and Alfred Grandidier.</p><p>Baobabs have religious and spiritual significance for many Malagasy tribes, although the majority of the island’s population will probably never see one – they only grow along the western margin of the island and not in the more populous central highlands. However, as the national tree and the national emblem, the trees are an ideal symbol for increasing awareness not only of the endangered forests, but also of the traditional beliefs of the people who live alongside them. But what does the future hold for the Malagasy baobabs?</p><p>The Avenue des Baobabs is something of a contradiction; the ‘natural’ scene that tourists visit is actually man-made. While the trees themselves are natural enough, the surrounding landscape was created by humans. Back in the early 20th century, much of the Morondava plain was cleared for rice and sugar-cane fields. However, many baobabs were spared because they are valuable as a food source and renewable building material, or they simply survived the fires that were set to clear the dry deciduous forest around them. These days many stand alone in fields, a reminder of where the forest once was. But now it seems that even the mighty baobabs are falling down. ‘Every year I see new trees that have fallen,’ says Jim Bond, an ethnobotanist who has studied Madagascar’s baobabs for more than a decade. The exact cause of the falling baobabs is uncertain, but some people think that the water used to irrigate the nearby fields is partly to blame. Baobabs are adapted to the annual wet season, but their roots can’t cope with constantly waterlogged soils. Lone trees separated from the surrounding forest are also susceptible to cyclones, which reach speeds of over 200 kph in the area, and can easily uproot even a 30-metre baobab.</p><p>Baobabs elsewhere on the island are also facing a host of threats, with three of the six native species now listed as endangered by the World Conservation Union. David Baum from the University of Wisconsin has spent years researching Malagasy baobabs. ‘The main threat to their survival is land clearance for farming, which probably suppresses regeneration,’ he says. Burning is the usual method of clearing land, and while baobabs have some resistance to this, they will eventually succumb to repeated burning. Human disturbance in the forest, such as logging, is also a problem. This practice may be promoting the rapid spread of shrubby undergrowth, which quickly fills in the bright forest gaps needed for baobab germination. Another problem the trees face is the dispersal of their seeds. Baum suggests that dispersal is being limited by the absence of two large, now extinct animals– a gorilla – sized lemur and the three-metre-tall elephant bird. Passage through the digestive tract of these giant fruit-eating animals probably once played a vital role in the distribution of the renala baobabs in particular.</p><p>The Malagasy government is becoming increasingly concerned as the baobab is key to the local tourism industry, and thus to the economy of the island as a whole. But visiting and photographing the solitary trees in the dramatic open landscape at the Avenue des Baobabs isn’t the only way to experience baobabs in Madagascar. Within a few hours&#x27; drive of Morondava, three species of baobab can be seen in largely intact, dry forest habitat. Jim Bond has led expeditions of Malagasy and foreign participants to the Mikea forest in the island’s southwest, which is almost certainly one of the least studied and least damaged areas of baobab forest left in Madagascar. On his expeditions Bond works closely with local people to search for, and create maps of baobab distribution. He also plans to compile an illustrated children’s book of the many local folk-tales about the tree, hoping that national and international versions will help to spread the word about how important baobabs are in Malagasy culture. But it’s going to require some swift and coordinated action to ensure that the baobab becomes a symbol of conservation success, rather than a symbol of humanity’s mistakes.</p><p><i>* Malagasy: of Madagascar</i></p>", "2": "<p>Few people pay much attention to coins; we take them for granted. Since the advent of paper currency and the abandonment of a monetary system based on intrinsic of actual value, they have been demoted to the status of change: what is left when you have paid for something with &#x27;paper&#x27; money. There is even a belief that they will one day be obsolete, as will paper or plastic notes, and money will at last have achieved the pure status of a mathematical formulation, a system of electronic credits and debits.</p><p>In the modern media environment, the sheer number of visual images is overwhelming, but it makes one stop to think that coins are among the most durable of our artefacts, and future archaeologists will scrutinise coins in order to learn about their distant ancestors. This is exactly the way ancient coins have informed us for over four millennia. There are thousands of different coins, each designed to carry meaning as well as value, tiny but rich sources of documentary evidence about events and cultural attitudes, frequently produced with exquisite craftsmanship.</p><p>Coins in Europe date from 2,500 to 2,700 years ago, and seem to have originated in the ancient kingdom of Lydia, in what is now Turkey. Before coins, trade was based on barter: the direct trade of commodities. Barter required the physical presence of both commodities that were being traded. Metal coins were far more portable, and not subject to the same spoilage on long sea journeys as a commodity like grain might be if it became wet. However, they could also be accumulated, and these hoards could be stolen. Coins were therefore a temptation to criminals. The Greeks, however, were the first to realise the full potential of the new invention. They were a trading people of seafarers. Their civilisation, which was composed of dozens of city-states, was highly decentralised. Despite the potential for theft, the Greeks realised that they could benefit enormously from a system that used coins, because it meant that they no longer had to carry goods with them to barter.</p><p>The value of coins was generally based on the intrinsic value of the metal they were made of. At first this was mostly silver, and this meant that the discovery of silver mines on Greek territory actually increased the quantity of money they could produce. Had the Greek mining been on a larger scale, it could have caused problems similar to those faced by the Spanish centuries later, when so much gold from the New World was brought into their economy that it resulted in serious inflation. One advantage of intrinsic value was that coins could be weighed to determine the amount of metal they contained, so that the only thing an issuing state had to guarantee was the purity of the metal to prevent accusations of fraud and cheating, which could cause fighting and unrest. This was quite difficult to do, especially with coins made of gold, until Archimedes realised that different metals have different weight-volume ratios, and that by measuring the amount of displacement when coins were immersed in water, it was possible to establish whether gold was alloyed with silver.</p><p>All ancient coins were made by hand using engraved metal moulds. Many hundreds of coins were made, and as a result the moulds often cracked and new ones had to be engraved. This explains the endless variety encountered in coins with the same designs because the moulds had to be remade repeatedly, by many different artisans. One of the distinctive features of coins, in fact, is that they are the work of anonymous craftsmen, and yet each coin is an intimate reflection of the character of its city of origin. It was the Greeks who realised that coins could have a double function, as a unit of value and as a symbol of the identity of the city that issued them. The Greek view of life was deeply motivated by a spirit of competition, and coins, which circulated around the trading world, were a way of advertising the qualities of each particular city-state.</p><p>Interestingly, Greek coins from the city of Athens, which was the home of the greatest sculptors of the classical period, seem to have been completely disconnected from the high sculptural tradition. The beautiful features of the city&#x27;s patron, Athena (with her owl on the reverse of the coin), seem to have been repeated virtually unaltered for many years. Perhaps this was because the Athenian currency had grown increasingly to be the international standard and, like the US dollar today, a uniform design implied integrity.</p><p>One side of a coin was always occupied by the head of a divinity, so it was a relatively small step, at around 323 BC, for Greek coins to replace that head with the portrait of the reigning monarch, a tradition that has continued in monarchies to the present day. Republics may have either symbolic heads – going back to the older tradition of divinities – or the features of a distinguished past head of state. The Romans adopted the Greek practice of coin design and as a result, the features of many great Romans have survived and are known to modern generations, despite the fact that the art of portrait painting was completely lost for more than a millennium following the fall of the Roman Empire.</p>", "3": "<p><b>A </b>There is a reason why grapefruit juice is served in little glasses. Most people don&#x27;t want to drink more than a few ounces at a time. This is because a natural chemical compound found in grapefruit, naringin, has a bitter taste. Some people like that bitterness in small doses and believe it enhances the general flavour, but others would rather avoid it altogether. So juice packagers often select grapefruit with low naringin content, even though the compound has antioxidant properties that some nutritionists contend may help prevent many serious diseases.</p><p><b>B</b> It is possible, however, to get the goodness of grapefruit without the bitter taste. In a test conducted by a biotechnology company, sets of two miniature white paper cups, labelled 304 and 305, were placed before five people seated around a conference table. Each person drank from one cup and then the other, cleansing their palates between tastes with water and a cracker. Even the smallest sip of 304 had grapefruit&#x27;s unmistakeable bite. But 305 was smoother; there was the sour taste of citrus but none of the bitterness of naringin. This juice had been treated with AMP, a compound that blocks the bitterness in foods without making them less nutritious.</p><p><b>C </b>Taste research is a booming business these days, with scientists delving into all five basics: sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami (the savory taste of protein). However, bitterness is of special interest to the food industry because it is present in so many different types of food. There are thousands of bitter-tasting compounds in nature. They defend plants by warning animals away and protect animals by letting them know when a plant may be poisonous. But the system isn&#x27;t foolproof. Grapefruit and bitter green vegetables like Brussels sprouts and kale are nutritious despite - and sometimes because of - their bitter-tasting components.</p><p><b>D</b> &#x27;Humans are the only species that enjoys a bitter taste,&#x27; says Charles Zuker, a neuroscientist at the University of California School of Medicine at San Diego. &#x27;Every other species is averse to bitter because it means bad news. But we have learned to enjoy it. We drink coffee, which is bitter, and quinine (in tonic water) too. We enjoy having that spice in our lives.&#x27; Because bitterness can be pleasant in small quantities but repellent when intense, bitter blockers like AMP could make a whole range of foods, drinks and medicines more palatable.</p><p><b>E </b>People have varying capacities for tasting bitterness and the differences appear to be genetic. About 75% of people are sensitive to the taste of some well-identified bitter compounds. Those who are sensitive seem to be less likely than others to eat bitter vegetables, according to Stephen Wooding, a geneticist at the University of Utah. Some people who have an unusually high number of taste buds, and are known as &#x27;supertasters&#x27;, are especially sensitive to these compounds. Supertasters tend to shun all kinds of bitter-tasting things, including vegetables, coffee, and dark chocolate.</p><p><b>F </b>The tongue is covered with taste buds. Under the microscope each one looks like an onion, consisting of 50 to 100 elongated cells running from the top of the taste buds to the bottom. At the top is a group of receptors that captures the taste molecules, known as tastants, in food and drink. The receptors function much like those for sight and smell. Once a bitter signal has been received, it is relayed via proteins known as G proteins. The G protein involved in the perception of bitterness, sweetness and umami was identified in the early 1990s by Robert Margolskee, at Mount Sinai School of Medicine in New York. The protein triggers a cascade of chemical reactions that lead to ion concentrations within the cell. Ultimately, this delivers a signal to the brain that registers as bitter.</p><p><b>G </b>Once they understood the taste mechanism, scientists began to think of ways to interfere with it. They tried AMP, an organic compound found in breast milk and other substances, that is created as cells break down food. AMP has no bitterness of its own, but when put in foods, as Margolskee and his colleagues discovered, it attaches to bitter-taste receptors. As effective as it is, AMP may not be able to dampen every type of bitter taste because it probably doesn&#x27;t attach to all 30 bitter-taste receptors.</p><p><b>H </b>Some taste researchers believe that, in time, compounds like AMP will help make processed foods less unhealthy. Consider, for example, that a single cup of canned chicken noodle soup contains 850 milligrams of sodium chloride, or table salt - more than a third of the recommended daily allowance. The salt masks the bitterness created by the high temperatures used in the canning process, which cause sugars and amino acids to react. Part of the table salt could be replaced by another type of salt, potassium chloride, which tends to be scarce in some people&#x27;s diets because of its bitter taste. But that bitter taste could be eliminated with a dose of AMP. Bitter blockers could also be used in place of sugary cherry and grape flavourings in children&#x27;s cough syrups, and they could dampen the bitterness of drugs like antihistamines, antibiotics and other similar products.</p><p><b>I </b>A number of foodmakers have already begun to experiment with AMP in their products, and other bitter blockers are being developed. In a few years, perhaps, after food companies have taken the bitterness from canned soup and improved the taste of medicines, they can set their sights on something more useful: a bitter blocker in a bottle that any of us can sprinkle on our Brussels sprouts or stir into our grapefruit juice.</p>"}, "questionSegments": {"1": ["r1-p1"], "2": ["r1-p1"], "3": ["r1-p2"], "4": ["r1-p3"], "5": ["r1-p3"], "6": ["r1-p3"], "7": ["r1-p3"], "8": ["r1-p3"], "9": ["r1-p4"], "10": ["r1-p4"], "11": ["r1-p4"], "12": ["r1-p5"], "13": ["r1-p5"], "14": ["r2-p1"], "15": ["r2-p2"], "16": ["r2-p3"], "17": ["r2-p4"], "18": ["r2-p5"], "19": ["r2-p6"], "20": ["r2-p7"], "21": ["r2-p5"], "22": ["r2-p5"], "23": ["r2-p3"], "24": ["r2-p3"], "25": ["r2-p4"], "26": ["r2-p4"], "27": ["r3-pB"], "28": ["r3-pH"], "29": ["r3-pE"], "30": ["r3-pF"], "31": ["r3-pC"], "32": ["r3-pH"], "33": ["r3-pD"], "34": ["r3-pA"], "35": ["r3-pC"], "36": ["r3-pE"], "37": ["r3-pE"], "38": ["r3-pF"], "39": ["r3-pF"], "40": ["r3-pG"]}, "headings": [{"value": "i", "text": "Training coin makers"}, {"value": "ii", "text": "A form of regional promotion"}, {"value": "iii", "text": "More gold than silver in Greece"}, {"value": "iv", "text": "Calculating what a coin was worth"}, {"value": "v", "text": "A simpler, more efficient way of trading"}, {"value": "vi", "text": "Putting a face to famous historical names"}, {"value": "vii", "text": "An uncertain future"}, {"value": "viii", "text": "Careless workmanship caused breakages"}, {"value": "ix", "text": "Unchanging coins signalled trustworthiness"}, {"value": "x", "text": "Reading the writing on coins"}, {"value": "xi", "text": "Significant records of past societies"}], "grading": {"pairs": [21]}, "groups": [[1, 13], [14, 26], [27, 40]]}};